WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.760
- Good afternoon, thank
you so much for coming

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.841
to the second annual Canvas
@ Yale lightning talks.

00:00:13.841 --> 00:00:17.030
I'm very excited to have a
bunch of great presenters today

00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:20.850
and I just want to thank
everyone who helped out

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.220
with the set up today, who
helped getting everybody ready

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:27.250
and this was a labor of love.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.830
Thank you so much to everyone who helped

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.840
make this happen here today.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.370
I want to jump right in and
get started on everything.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.470
So the first thing here, we have Julia,

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.480
who's going to be our presenter.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.360
Julia is a senior lector
in the Slavic department

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.193
and is a Canvas quiz guru.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.120
Aside from teaching Julia
has published articles

00:00:51.073 --> 00:00:52.860
in a lot of national peer review journals

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.870
and is also interested in
working with technology,

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:59.430
digital humanities and
heritage language learning.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:01.730
Today she's going to be talking
about how she integrated

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.623
the Canvas quizzes into her course.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:06.290
- Thank you so much.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:09.010
Thank you for coming, it's great to see

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:11.960
so many familiar faces and
some of you teach language too

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.910
so I hope that you would find
this presentation helpful

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.250
because as I was telling
Timberley language teachers

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.083
invariably do a lot of grading.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:25.040
I will tell you a little
bit about the structure

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.650
of this course and why I
find Canvas so so helpful.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.400
So I teach and oversee
introductory Russian,

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.350
first year Russian.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.860
As many introductory
language courses at Yale

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:37.330
it has multiple sections.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:41.180
So I'm the course coordinator,
I oversee multiple sections.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.940
I get every year new teaching assistants

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.960
who have never taught before

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.610
so obviously one of the paramount tasks

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:51.570
is to ensure that grading is consistent

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.370
across multiple sections.

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:58.300
As anybody who has been in
a similar situation knows

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:02.580
it is very difficult for
an experienced teacher

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.370
to teach a new teacher to
grade exactly like yourself

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:08.320
because it's not just
finding the correct answer

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.500
that you can teach them to
do, you will give the key,

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:11.760
that's not the problem.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.170
But to make sure how certain
components of the test

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:17.280
are evaluated as a whole.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.860
Is it worth one point,
is it worth half a point,

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.560
is it considered a spelling error,

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.250
is it considered repeated error
in which case you would not

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:25.103
and so forth.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:30.090
Again, the other challenge that we have

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.890
in introductory language classes,

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:35.160
we want to give these quizzes
and exams fairly often.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.200
We cannot teach a class where
we'll just give a midterm

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.250
and the final, that would
have disastrous results.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.670
I know that majority of
my colleagues and as do we

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.630
in Russian, we have bi-weekly unit tests.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.080
That means they're graded,
students get the letter

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.730
or in Canvas cases a percentage grade

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:56.240
and on the weeks that are
off weeks from the tests,

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.520
we have what we call low stakes quizzes.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.120
That is to say this would also be a quiz

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.800
but it will be shorter and
instead of a letter grade

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.710
you will have a percentage.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:06.833
Why do we do that?

00:03:07.681 --> 00:03:10.010
We fill that in the introductory
level it's very important

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.240
to have this what we call level checks

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.460
or progress measurements.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.560
Because if student gets a very low score

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.740
on this interim low stake quiz

00:03:20.898 --> 00:03:22.890
then it tells them okay well
the material in this unit

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.080
really needs more work so
by the time they approach

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.680
the unit test they will have
already had that feedback.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.465
Again, I know that this is something

00:03:32.465 --> 00:03:35.610
that I see Sherry in Hebrew
and Angela in Korean,

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.550
we all have this approach.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:41.390
It's very important to give
the student frequent feedback.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:44.223
At the same time, in paper testing,

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:48.240
it also in addition to obvious
challenges for uniformity

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.750
of grading as I said,
there was another challenge

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:54.420
of simply if you correct mistake by hand

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.710
the student may or may
not be able to decipher

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:57.543
what you wrote.

00:03:58.475 --> 00:04:00.180
It's a huge problem in
the larger assignments

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.550
because each test invariably
has a free writing portion

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.750
and I'll show you in a minute.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.420
Whether they are able
to read your corrections

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.250
God only knows.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:11.490
Whether they read it
you don't know either.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.100
So in Canvas it's taken
care of beautifully

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.560
because you can type your feedback

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.270
and student will immediately see it.

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:21.100
You will also set up the
test and I will show you,

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.590
where the correct answers
are immediately shown.

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:25.880
We've used this feature

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.490
typically we use this
feature because at Yale

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.250
fortunately we don't have a problem

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:34.600
with academic dishonesty
so once you hit submit

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:38.120
you will immediately see
just like you do now on SAT.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.900
You will have your own score.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.850
By own score I mean of
course then I will invariably

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:45.260
go into the exam and
make sure that machine

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.710
has subtracted the points correctly.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:50.650
There is also free writing
portion of each test

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:53.850
which the Canvas can
now currently not grade

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:57.603
but I will grade by hand
and I will leave comments.

00:04:59.603 --> 00:05:02.720
For us, using Canvas in first year Russian

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.060
has proven to be of tremendous benefit

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:08.040
both on the learners progress,

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.930
because then it assures transparency,

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:11.850
students see exactly what went wrong.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:15.160
On consistency of grading,
then there are no more debates

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:17.390
or you've taken for this,
you didn't take for that

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:19.670
and so forth, it cross sections.

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:21.860
Of course, the frequent feedback.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:24.540
Finally, there is a great
organizational benefit

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.290
because we've all been in the situation

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:29.720
when student is sick or
they have to go out of town

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.900
in other words they
have a legitimate excuse

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:34.160
that they cannot take
their test with a group

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.690
and then you have to
somehow make arrangements

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:39.120
so that they have to come
in during the office hours

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.270
and even on organization
level it's very difficult

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.960
because you have to
find mutually convenient

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:44.843
time to proctor.

00:05:45.776 --> 00:05:47.670
Again, Canvas takes care of it wonderfully

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:50.110
because you as the teacher if you know

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:53.750
the reason is legitimate you
can open the test on demand.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:56.313
Which sometimes we do
and it works beautifully.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:58.343
What else we do?

00:05:59.178 --> 00:06:01.490
Presume your student
did very poorly on exam

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:04.180
and it's up to the teacher to decide

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:06.043
how you want to approach it.

00:06:06.043 --> 00:06:08.280
Presume somebody got very stressed

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:12.790
or they had some sort of issue
and they got very low score

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:17.110
then you can go into the test
and allow second attempt.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.190
It will be measured.

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:20.750
So now I will demonstrate for you,

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:22.290
I mean I put on the

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:26.880
abstract that I will
show you my full semester

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.110
but that doesn't really matter

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:29.400
because I am working with this now.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.220
So this is my second semester of this.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:33.590
Again I've used it last year too

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:36.490
so it's the second year or
the fourth semester of this.

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:38.570
So how it works, so quizzes.

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.760
So the children are not confused.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:43.720
So each chapter is
organized into these modules

00:06:45.566 --> 00:06:48.050
so you can see in January
we started with unit seven.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:49.430
There is a vocabulary quiz

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.720
because I've said when they will do.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.130
And then there is a chapter test

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.330
which is invariably last.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:58.800
So what do I do with a unit
test, low stake quizzes?

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.130
All quizzes are worth only
ten percent of total grade

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:04.130
so again it's that low stake testing

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:07.330
which research has shown
has tremendous benefits

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:10.230
because you want to test without students

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:11.713
being too stressed about it.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:15.863
So I will preview so you see
what the test looks like.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:18.120
I don't know if I can go

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:21.430
and show in the privacy
interest the quiz statistics.

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:23.650
So it looks like this.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:26.270
You can set it up, it's just
a simple vocabulary quiz.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:29.040
Everybody does it, it's very standard.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:30.870
So from English into Russian

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:32.483
then you have some components,

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:37.590
well on this one it's only
from English into Russian

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.450
but usually I set up two ways.

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:41.250
If it's a vocabulary quiz it will be

00:07:42.231 --> 00:07:43.064
from into the target language

00:07:43.064 --> 00:07:45.413
then from the target
language back into English.

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:48.823
You can of course change it at any time.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:54.010
What is super nice about this

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:57.480
if you want to see how people
did in the particular section

00:07:58.765 --> 00:08:01.790
or across the board if
your quiz was set up well

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:03.220
you go into this

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:08.770
and it will show you overall performance

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:10.470
so you see people did pretty good.

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.510
I don't know if it shows
me the average score, 90%.

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:14.393
Very nice.

00:08:16.435 --> 00:08:18.610
Obviously it was set up well.

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:19.810
I'll show you one more thing

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:22.300
because I get the card for questions.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:24.640
I'll show you the test,
you want to see the test.

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:26.240
A little bit, just one,

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:28.090
because they do say they want to see.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:35.823
So grading is beautiful.

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:39.690
Listening of course,
nobody has any problems

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:40.680
with listening.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:42.883
So green you don't worry about the green.

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:49.020
So red, of course, you have
to see how Canvas scores it.

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:51.670
So here there was nothing for me to do

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:54.280
because I went in and there was issues,

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.000
there were various issues here,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:59.080
it's not the best test to show of course.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:02.440
But in any case, so the
green one you don't even

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:04.320
have to worry, it's tremendous time saver

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:06.010
because green you just scroll.

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:07.563
Red one you should see.

00:09:08.466 --> 00:09:11.130
Like here you see I
awarded two and half points

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:13.660
because I gave you back
half the credit for here

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:15.700
because there is a spelling error.

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:19.160
So on the red one you
look and then you go,

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:20.990
again this is a very poor example to show

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:22.940
because this is very bad work,

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:24.503
but this is the free writing.

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:28.973
So this is the free writing
for the free writing prompt,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.750
so students write then I can,

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:35.160
when I get here I say
look this is always worth

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:37.350
14 out of possible 20 points,

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:40.020
then I tell her here why it is

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:41.860
because I have this comment, you see,

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:44.143
where I correct her issues.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:47.510
Here for overall comment
I didn't say anything

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:50.333
but it allows you to put the comments in.

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:54.050
This work was turned
in late, you see that,

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:55.660
so it's very nice.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:59.661
If you have multiple
attempts it will show you.

00:09:59.661 --> 00:10:01.460
Well this is obviously
a very good work 99.5.

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:07.750
It's wonderful because then they can see

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:10.640
immediately the problems,
what they need to work

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:11.850
on and improve.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:14.180
Again I can go back to quiz statistics

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:18.323
and I will see how the sections did.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:20.683
Questions?

00:10:30.605 --> 00:10:32.300
- [Woman] Are these in
class tests and quizzes

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.940
or are these taken online?

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:37.720
- So unit tests, the one
that you're looking at now,

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:39.960
will be taken in class
because there is also

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.330
a listening component that I will play,

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.361
that they don't have listened to before,

00:10:44.361 --> 00:10:45.576
this will be played in class.

00:10:45.576 --> 00:10:46.580
So anything that they get a letter grade

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:48.765
and these are weighted heavier

00:10:48.765 --> 00:10:51.340
because unit tests count
for 40% of the grade.

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:53.200
So yes I will do that in class.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:57.030
Vocabulary quizzes which was
the first item demonstrated,

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:59.530
vocabulary quizzes are
considered low stake item

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:03.420
and those will be done out
of class, out of class time.

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:07.690
But that's a good point because
what I intend to do later,

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:09.170
like maybe next academic year,

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:11.310
I want to add more of
these low stake quizzes.

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:14.110
So rather than giving
one a week maybe I'll

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:14.990
give a couple a week.

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:18.728
Because how else can you
make them learn vocabulary.

00:11:18.728 --> 00:11:20.873
You've seen that problem.

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:31.670
- [Man] Who set up your Canvas presence?

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:34.510
Did you do it yourself or
did you hire an expert?

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:38.570
- Well I had great help
from Christine Constantina

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:40.263
and so we worked together.

00:11:40.263 --> 00:11:43.316
She was very generous with her time

00:11:43.316 --> 00:11:46.160
and thanking both Timberly and Christine

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:47.823
have really helped tremendously.

00:11:48.947 --> 00:11:50.860
Now every time I have a
problem because I still do

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:53.103
I ask Christine and she helps me.

00:11:54.054 --> 00:11:56.120
The more you use it the better it gets.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:57.770
The first time I thought it was horrible

00:11:57.770 --> 00:12:00.033
because every answer is a new screen.

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:06.050
But eventually you get the hang of it.

00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:10.030
Again, truth be told it is
time consuming to set up

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:11.870
but it's absolutely well worth it

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:15.040
because last year we spent
tremendous amount of time

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:16.950
setting it up but now it's all there

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:20.250
so now I can concentrate
on more define and adding

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:22.650
because also I'm sure
somebody will talk about it,

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:25.200
Canvas has this beautiful
button copy resources

00:12:26.372 --> 00:12:27.630
of which I can happily
talk about next time

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:30.540
because resources are
much easier to maintain

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:32.290
and copy from semester to semester.

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:35.833
I'm a huge fan of this
program, absolutely.

00:12:42.176 --> 00:12:43.410
- [Woman] So for the
order of the questions

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:46.470
can you shuffle words or order?

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:50.560
- You can, for my particular
situation it's not practical

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:51.850
but yes absolutely.

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:54.680
When you set up the quiz
you can absolutely do.

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:57.910
You can have bank of
questions and it will pick

00:12:57.910 --> 00:12:59.480
from the bank.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:02.320
For us, because the first
year on introductory languages

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:04.020
are so sequential,

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:06.050
I can do it in my other class for example,

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:07.970
I can advance to whatever heritage.

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:10.370
But in the introductory language level

00:13:12.351 --> 00:13:13.320
the points that you wish to test

